cerberus (0.10.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Repair the 0.10.5 changelog entry, which was missing its trailer line. dpkg
    parsed it with no Date or Maintainer field and warned, and lintian reported
    syntax-error-in-debian-changelog against all four binary packages, each of
    which ships this file as changelog.gz.
  * RPM-side fix carried in the same release for version parity: the RPM
    packages were uninstallable on rpm >= 6 (current Fedora) because %files
    entries naming the cerberus/cerberus-audit accounts generate
    Requires: user()/group(), and only a packaged sysusers.d file provides them.
    The RPM now ships sysusers.d fragments as Debian already did. No change to
    the Debian packages themselves.
  * Committed version fields in the RPM spec and the Arch PKGBUILD are now
    obvious placeholders rather than stale real-looking versions, so a build run
    without the wrapper scripts fails visibly instead of producing a package
    labelled with a previous release.
  * Dependency updates: aws-sdk group bumped in ssh-cert-api and
    ssh-cert-signer.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0400

cerberus (0.10.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the config template at /usr/share/cerberus/config.yaml.example
    instead of /etc/cerberus/config.yaml.example. Nothing reads the file --
    operators copy it to config.yaml -- so shipping it under /etc made it a
    dpkg conffile, which froze it: once an admin touched it, upgrades stopped
    refreshing it and newly added options never surfaced. As read-only package
    data under /usr/share it is replaced on every upgrade, matching the RPM
    and Arch packaging. cerberus-api.maintscript retires the old conffile;
    a locally modified copy is preserved as .dpkg-bak.
  * The template now ships 0644 rather than 0640. A restricted mode is
    anomalous under /usr/share and bought nothing here: the file carries no
    secrets and is public in the source repository. Its lintian override is
    dropped accordingly; the env files keep 0640 and their override.
  * cerberus-api continues to own /etc/cerberus (0750 root:cerberus), which
    was previously created only as a side effect of installing the template
    into it. That is still where the operator's config.yaml belongs.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 18:00:00 -0400

cerberus (0.10.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Reduce cerberus-vsock-watch alert noise on a cerberus-api.service
    restart: a third real restart chaos test on the same RHEL 10 host
    confirmed 0.10.3's Indeterminate downgrade was working correctly
    (the reported failure was a bug in the verification script, since
    fixed) but the Indeterminate alert still fired on essentially every
    restart -- safe but noisy. Allowlist.Classify's single cgroup
    recheck is now a bounded retry loop instead of one attempt, so the
    common case resolves cleanly to Expected with no alert at all. See
    docs/vsock-connect-detection.md §4.1.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Thu, 23 Jul 2026 17:30:00 -0400

cerberus (0.10.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a second round of a real false-positive in cerberus-vsock-watch,
    found via a second real cerberus-api.service restart chaos test on a
    real RHEL 10 host: 0.10.2's uncached recheck reduced but did not
    fully close a cgroup-mismatch false positive, since a single
    immediate recheck can still race systemd's own cgroup settling
    during a restart. Allowlist.Classify now downgrades a cgroup
    mismatch that survives the recheck to Indeterminate (alerts, never
    auto-kills) instead of trying to outrun the timing race. Applies to
    this Debian package the same as RPM and Arch. See
    docs/vsock-connect-detection.md §4.1.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Thu, 23 Jul 2026 16:00:00 -0400

cerberus (0.10.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a real false-positive in cerberus-vsock-watch found via a live
    cerberus-api.service restart chaos test on a real RHEL 10 host
    (verify-vsock-watch-hardware.sh): Allowlist's cached expected cgroup
    inode can go stale across a restart that changes the unit's cgroup,
    misclassifying the newly-restarted, legitimate ssh-cert-api as
    Anomalous for up to the 5s cache window -- and, with the opt-in
    --block reactive-kill enabled, killing it outright. Allowlist.Classify
    now does one uncached re-check before declaring a uid or cgroup
    mismatch Anomalous. Applies to this Debian package the same as RPM
    and Arch, since it's a Go code fix, not packaging. See
    docs/vsock-connect-detection.md §4.1.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Thu, 23 Jul 2026 14:30:00 -0400

cerberus (0.10.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * No Debian-specific changes; version bump to stay in lockstep with the
    RPM/Arch packaging, which fixed cerberus-vsock-watch missing auditctl on
    RHEL 10 (that release moved it into a separate audit-rules package). The
    Debian cerberus-vsock-watch package's Depends: audit is unaffected.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Thu, 23 Jul 2026 09:00:00 -0400

cerberus (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New cerberus-vsock-watch package: a detective control for SIGN-1
    (docs/THREAT-MODEL.md) — alerts on any AF_VSOCK connect() to the enclave
    from a process other than cerberus-api, via two independent detectors
    (an auditd rule and an eBPF tracepoint probe). Does not close the
    underlying gap; makes exploitation observable. See
    docs/vsock-connect-detection.md.

 -- Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>  Wed, 22 Jul 2026 09:50:00 -0400

